    "title": "Algorithms and Big-O Complexity",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Algorithm analysis",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how the running time of an algorithm grows with the size of its input. Use examples, explain why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "An algorithm is a finite, precise sequence of steps that solves a problem. Two algorithms can give the same answer yet differ greatly in how much time or memory they need, and the difference matters most when the input is large.",
      "Computer scientists describe efficiency by how the number of steps grows as the input size n grows, written in Big-O notation. Big-O keeps only the fastest-growing term and drops constant factors, because for large n the growth rate dominates: an algorithm taking 3n + 5 steps is O(n), and one taking n squared plus 100n is O(n²). Big-O is about growth, not about the exact time on a particular computer.",
      "Common classes, from fastest to slowest growing, are O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic, O(n) linear, O(n log n), O(n²) quadratic and O(2ⁿ) exponential. Doubling the input barely changes a logarithmic algorithm, doubles a linear one, and quadruples a quadratic one; exponential algorithms become impractical even for modest inputs.",
      "Searching shows the difference. Linear search checks items one by one and takes O(n) steps in the worst case. Binary search works only on sorted data: it compares the target with the middle item and discards half the remaining items each time, so it needs O(log n) steps, about 20 comparisons for a million items. Analysts usually state the worst case, because it is a guarantee; the average case can be better, and the best case is rarely informative."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "big-o-meaning", "title": "Explain what Big-O notation measures", "description": "Describes Big-O as the growth rate of work with input size and why constants are dropped.",
        "concept": "Big-O notation", "definition": "Big-O describes how an algorithm's steps grow as input size grows, keeping only the dominant term.",
        "facts": ["Big-O describes growth of steps or memory as input size n grows.", "Constant factors and lower-order terms are dropped.", "For large inputs the growth rate dominates the running time."],
        "misconceptions": ["Big-O gives the exact running time in seconds.", "An O(n) algorithm is always faster than an O(n²) one for every input size."] },
      { "key": "complexity-classes", "title": "Compare common complexity classes", "description": "Orders constant, logarithmic, linear, n log n, quadratic and exponential growth and says what doubling the input does.",
        "concept": "Complexity classes", "definition": "Standard growth rates ordered from slowest to fastest growing: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ).",
        "facts": ["Doubling n roughly doubles the work of an O(n) algorithm.", "Doubling n roughly quadruples the work of an O(n²) algorithm.", "Exponential algorithms become impractical even for modest inputs."],
        "misconceptions": ["Logarithmic growth is slower than linear only for small inputs."] },
      { "key": "search-comparison", "title": "Contrast linear search and binary search", "description": "Explains why binary search is O(log n), what it requires, and why linear search is O(n).",
        "concept": "Linear and binary search", "definition": "Linear search checks items one at a time; binary search halves a sorted range at each step.",
