    "title": "Recursion and Divide and Conquer",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Recursion",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how recursive solutions work and when they are a good idea. Talk through examples step by step, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "A recursive function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller instance of the same problem. Every correct recursion has two parts: a base case, which is answered directly without another call, and a recursive case, which reduces the problem toward the base case. Factorial is the classic example: 0! is 1 (the base case), and n! is n times (n − 1)! (the recursive case).",
      "Each call that has not yet finished is kept on the call stack, with its own parameters and local variables. Calls pile up until a base case is reached, then return one by one, each finishing its own work with the result of the call it made. If the base case is missing or never reached, calls pile up until the stack runs out of space, a stack overflow.",
      "Divide and conquer is a recursive strategy: split the problem into smaller subproblems, solve each recursively, and combine the results. Merge sort splits a list in half, sorts each half recursively, and merges the two sorted halves; because the list can be halved only about log n times and each level of merging does O(n) work, merge sort runs in O(n log n).",
      "Recursion can be wasteful when subproblems repeat. Computing Fibonacci numbers by calling fib(n − 1) and fib(n − 2) recomputes the same values again and again, taking exponential time. Memoization stores each answer the first time it is computed and looks it up afterwards, cutting the work to linear time. Any recursive algorithm can also be written with loops and an explicit stack; recursion is chosen when it makes the solution clearer."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "base-recursive-case", "title": "Explain the base case and the recursive case", "description": "Identifies both parts of a recursion and explains why the problem must shrink toward the base case.",
