    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how common data structures organize data and what each is good and bad at. Give examples of when you would use each, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "A data structure is a way of organizing data in memory so that certain operations are fast. No structure is best at everything; choosing one means trading the cost of some operations against others.",
      "An array stores items in one contiguous block of memory, so any item can be reached directly by its index in constant time, O(1). Inserting or deleting in the middle is slow, O(n), because later items must shift. A linked list stores each item in a node that points to the next node; inserting or removing next to a node you already hold is O(1), but reaching the k-th item means following pointers from the start, which is O(n).",
      "Stacks and queues restrict how items are added and removed. A stack is last in, first out (LIFO): push adds to the top and pop removes from the top, as with an undo history or the call stack. A queue is first in, first out (FIFO): items join at the back and leave from the front, as with a print queue or tasks waiting for a processor.",
      "A hash table stores key-value pairs. A hash function turns each key into an index in an underlying array, so lookup, insertion and deletion take O(1) time on average. Two keys can hash to the same index, a collision, which the table handles by chaining or probing; with many collisions performance degrades toward O(n). A binary search tree keeps smaller keys in the left subtree and larger keys in the right, so search takes O(log n) when the tree is balanced, but a tree built from already-sorted input can degenerate into a chain with O(n) search."
    ],
