  {
    "title": "How the Internet Routes Packets",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Networks",
    "topic": "Internet routing",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how data travels across the internet from one computer to another. Describe what happens along the way and why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The internet is a network of networks that uses packet switching. Data such as a file or a video stream is split into small packets, each carrying a header with its source and destination addresses. Packets travel independently, can take different paths, and share links with traffic from many other users, unlike a telephone circuit that reserves one path for a whole call.",
      "Every device on the internet is identified by an IP address. Routers forward packets hop by hop: each router reads the destination address, looks it up in its routing table, and sends the packet to the next router that is closer to the destination. No single router knows the full path. Routers exchange routing information so they can adapt when links fail or become congested; between the large networks run by different organizations, called autonomous systems, this is done with the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP). Each packet also carries a time-to-live count that drops at every hop, so a packet caught in a loop is eventually discarded.",
      "IP itself is best effort: packets may be lost, duplicated or delivered out of order. TCP, which runs on top of IP, builds a reliable connection from this. It numbers the bytes it sends, the receiver acknowledges what arrives, missing data is retransmitted, and the receiver puts data back in order. TCP also slows down when the network is congested. UDP skips these guarantees for lower delay, which suits live voice, video and games, where late data is useless.",
      "Networking is organized in layers. The link layer moves frames across one physical link such as Wi-Fi or Ethernet; the internet layer (IP) moves packets across networks; the transport layer (TCP or UDP) delivers data between programs on the two hosts; and the application layer holds protocols such as HTTP and email. Each layer relies on the one below without needing to know how it works."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "packet-switching", "title": "Explain packet switching", "description": "Explains why data is split into packets that travel independently over shared links.",
        "concept": "Packet switching", "definition": "Data is split into addressed packets that travel independently over shared links.",
        "facts": ["Data is broken into small packets with headers.", "Each header holds source and destination addresses.", "Packets of one message can take different paths.", "Links are shared rather than reserved for one conversation."],
        "misconceptions": ["The internet reserves a dedicated wire between two computers.", "A file always travels as one piece."] },
      { "key": "ip-routing", "title": "Describe how routers forward packets", "description": "Explains IP addresses, routing tables, hop-by-hop forwarding and adapting to failures.",
        "concept": "IP routing", "definition": "Routers forward each packet one hop closer to its destination IP address using routing tables.",
        "facts": ["Every device is identified by an IP address.", "Each router chooses only the next hop using its routing table.", "No single router knows the whole path.", "Routers exchange routes so traffic can avoid failed links."],
        "misconceptions": ["The sender chooses the complete route in advance.", "One central computer directs all internet traffic."] },
      { "key": "tcp-udp", "title": "Explain how TCP makes delivery reliable and when UDP is used", "description": "Describes acknowledgements, retransmission and reordering, and the trade-off UDP makes.",
        "concept": "TCP and UDP", "definition": "TCP adds reliable, ordered delivery on top of best-effort IP; UDP sends without those guarantees for lower delay.",
        "facts": ["IP is best effort and can lose or reorder packets.", "TCP numbers data and the receiver acknowledges it.", "TCP retransmits lost data and restores order.", "UDP suits live audio, video and games where late data is useless."],
        "misconceptions": ["IP guarantees every packet arrives.", "UDP is simply a broken version of TCP."] },
      { "key": "layering", "title": "Explain why networking is organized in layers", "description": "Names the main layers and explains how each depends on the one below.",
        "concept": "Protocol layering", "definition": "Network functions are split into layers, each using the services of the layer below.",
        "facts": ["The link layer moves data across one physical link.", "The internet layer moves packets across networks.", "The transport layer delivers data between programs.", "Application protocols such as HTTP sit on top."],
        "misconceptions": ["The internet and the World Wide Web are the same thing."] }
    ],
