    "title": "How the Web Works: DNS and HTTP",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Networks",
    "topic": "The World Wide Web",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain what happens between typing a web address and seeing the page. Explain each step and why it is needed, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The World Wide Web is a service that runs on the internet: documents and other resources identified by URLs and fetched with the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP). A URL such as https://www.example.com/news names the protocol (https), the host (www.example.com) and the path of the resource on that host (/news).",
      "Computers route traffic by IP address, so a host name must first be translated. The Domain Name System (DNS) does this. It is a distributed, hierarchical directory: root servers point to servers for top-level domains such as .com, which point to the authoritative servers for example.com, which hold its addresses. Answers are cached by resolvers and browsers for a time set by the record's time to live, so most lookups never reach the root.",
      "HTTP is a request-response protocol. The browser sends a request with a method, such as GET to fetch a resource or POST to submit data, a path, and headers. The server returns a status code, headers and usually a body: 200 means OK, 404 means the resource was not found, and 500 means the server itself failed. A web page usually needs many requests, one for the HTML and more for the images, style sheets and scripts it refers to.",
      "HTTP is stateless: each request is handled on its own, and the server does not automatically remember earlier ones. Sites remember a logged-in user or a shopping cart with cookies, small values the server asks the browser to store and send back with later requests. HTTPS is HTTP sent over an encrypted TLS connection, which keeps the content private and lets the browser check that it is talking to the real server."
    ],
