      "A relational database stores data in tables. Each table describes one kind of thing, such as students or courses; each row is one record and each column is one attribute, such as a name or a date of birth. A primary key is a column, or set of columns, whose value uniquely identifies each row, such as a student ID.",
      "Tables are linked by foreign keys: a column in one table that holds the primary key of a row in another. An enrollments table might hold a student ID and a course ID in each row, recording which student takes which course. A join combines rows from tables whose keys match, for example to list each student's name next to the titles of their courses.",
      "Data is queried with SQL, a declarative language: a query states what result is wanted, such as all students enrolled in Biology sorted by name, and the database decides how to find it. An index on a column works like the index of a book, letting the database find matching rows without scanning the whole table; indexes speed up reads but take space and slow down writes, because they must be updated too.",
      "Good design avoids storing the same fact in several places. Normalization splits data into tables so each fact is stored once; otherwise updating a teacher's phone number in one row but not another leaves the data contradicting itself. Databases also group changes into transactions with the ACID properties: atomic (all of the changes happen or none do), consistent (rules are never left broken), isolated (concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-finished work) and durable (once committed, changes survive a crash). A bank transfer that debits one account and credits another must be one transaction."
