    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how encryption, hashing and digital signatures keep information safe. Explain what each one protects against and why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Encryption turns readable plaintext into ciphertext using an algorithm and a key, so that only someone with the right key can turn it back. Modern systems assume the algorithm is public and keep only the key secret, a principle named after Auguste Kerckhoffs; security that depends on hiding the method breaks as soon as the method leaks.",
      "In symmetric encryption, such as AES, the same secret key encrypts and decrypts. It is fast, but the two parties must somehow share the key without an eavesdropper learning it. Public-key, or asymmetric, encryption, such as RSA, solves this with a key pair: a public key that anyone may know and use to encrypt, and a private key, kept secret, that alone can decrypt. Its security rests on problems that are believed to be very hard, such as factoring the product of two very large primes.",
      "Public-key encryption is much slower, so real systems combine the two. In TLS, the protocol behind HTTPS, the two sides use public-key methods to agree on a fresh symmetric session key, then encrypt the conversation itself with that fast symmetric key.",
      "A cryptographic hash function turns any input into a fixed-size digest. It is one-way, so the input cannot feasibly be recovered from the digest, and a tiny change to the input changes the digest completely. Hashing is not encryption, because there is no key and nothing to decrypt. Sites should store salted hashes of passwords rather than the passwords themselves. A digital signature is made with the signer's private key and checked with their public key; it shows who signed a message and that the message has not been changed since."
    ],
