        "facts": ["Symmetric encryption uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt.", "Sharing a symmetric key safely is a hard problem.", "In public-key encryption anyone can encrypt with the public key.", "Only the private key can decrypt."],
        "misconceptions": ["The public key must be kept secret.", "Knowing the public key lets you decrypt messages."] },
      { "key": "kerckhoffs-hybrid", "title": "Explain why the key, not the method, is secret and how TLS combines methods", "description": "States Kerckhoffs's principle and explains why TLS uses public-key methods to set up a symmetric session key.",
        "concept": "Kerckhoffs's principle and hybrid encryption", "definition": "Security should rest only on the secrecy of the key; practical systems use slow public-key methods to set up a fast symmetric key.",
        "facts": ["Algorithms are assumed public; only the key is secret.", "Public-key encryption is much slower than symmetric encryption.", "TLS agrees on a symmetric session key using public-key methods.", "The conversation itself is encrypted with the symmetric key."],
        "misconceptions": ["Keeping the encryption algorithm secret is what makes it secure."] },
