  {
    "title": "How a Computer Runs a Program",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Computer architecture",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain what happens inside a computer when it runs a program. Explain how the parts work together and why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Most computers follow the stored-program, or von Neumann, design: the instructions of a program and the data it works on are both kept as binary numbers in the same main memory. The central processing unit (CPU) contains a control unit that directs each step, an arithmetic logic unit (ALU) that does arithmetic and comparisons, and a few registers, very small and very fast storage locations inside the processor.",
      "The CPU runs a program with the fetch-decode-execute cycle. The program counter holds the memory address of the next instruction. The CPU fetches the instruction from that address, decodes it to work out what operation it asks for, and executes it, for example adding two registers or loading a value from memory. The program counter then moves to the next instruction, unless the instruction was a jump or branch that sets it elsewhere, which is how loops and if-statements work. A modern processor repeats this cycle billions of times per second.",
      "The CPU only understands machine code, the instruction set of its own processor family. Programs written in high-level languages must be translated. A compiler translates the whole program into machine code before it runs; an interpreter reads and carries out the program statement by statement while it runs. Many languages mix the two, compiling to an intermediate bytecode that a virtual machine runs.",
      "Memory is organized as a hierarchy. Registers are fastest but tiny; cache memory, on or near the processor, is fast and small; main memory (RAM) is larger and slower; and storage such as an SSD is far larger, far slower and keeps its contents without power. Caches work because programs show locality: they tend to reuse data they used recently and data stored next to it, so keeping that data close to the processor avoids most slow trips to main memory."
    ],
    "objectives": [
