        "facts": ["Instructions and data are both stored in main memory as binary.", "The control unit directs each step of execution.", "The ALU performs arithmetic and comparisons.", "Registers are small, very fast storage inside the CPU."],
        "misconceptions": ["The program runs directly from the hard drive without being loaded into memory.", "The CPU understands programming languages such as Python directly."] },
      { "key": "fetch-decode-execute", "title": "Explain the fetch-decode-execute cycle", "description": "Walks through the cycle and explains how the program counter and jumps make loops and decisions possible.",
        "concept": "Fetch-decode-execute cycle", "definition": "The CPU repeatedly fetches the instruction at the program counter, decodes it and executes it.",
        "facts": ["The program counter holds the address of the next instruction.", "The CPU fetches, decodes and then executes each instruction.", "Jump and branch instructions change the program counter.", "Loops and if-statements are built from branches."],
        "misconceptions": ["The CPU runs every line of the program at the same moment."] },
      { "key": "compile-interpret", "title": "Distinguish compilers from interpreters", "description": "Explains why high-level code must be translated to machine code and the two ways of doing it.",
        "concept": "Compilation and interpretation", "definition": "A compiler translates a whole program to machine code before running; an interpreter carries it out statement by statement.",
        "facts": ["The CPU runs only machine code for its instruction set.", "A compiler translates the whole program before it runs.", "An interpreter executes the program statement by statement as it runs.", "Some languages compile to bytecode run by a virtual machine."],
        "misconceptions": ["Interpreted programs are not translated at all."] },
      { "key": "memory-hierarchy", "title": "Explain the memory hierarchy and why caches work", "description": "Orders registers, cache, RAM and storage by speed and size and explains locality.",
        "concept": "Memory hierarchy", "definition": "Storage levels trade speed against size: registers, cache, main memory, then long-term storage.",
        "facts": ["Faster memory is smaller and more expensive per byte.", "Cache holds recently used data close to the processor.", "Programs tend to reuse recent and nearby data, called locality.", "RAM loses its contents without power; SSDs and disks do not."],
        "misconceptions": ["RAM and storage are the same thing.", "Adding more storage space makes programs run faster."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
