  {
    "title": "Operating Systems: Processes and Memory",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Operating systems",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how an operating system lets many programs share one computer safely. Explain what the operating system does and why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "An operating system (OS) manages a computer's hardware and gives programs a simpler, shared view of it. Its core, the kernel, runs with full control of the hardware, while ordinary programs run in a restricted mode and must ask the kernel, through system calls, to do things like read a file, send network data or start another program. This keeps one faulty or malicious program from taking over the machine.",
      "A process is a program in execution: its code, its own memory, and the state of its work, such as the current instruction and register values. A single process can contain several threads, which run separately but share the process's memory. Sharing makes communication between threads easy but risky: if two threads update the same data at once, the result can depend on timing, a race condition, so programs use locks to let only one thread change shared data at a time.",
      "A processor core runs one thread at a time, yet many programs appear to run at once. The OS scheduler gives each runnable thread a short time slice, then performs a context switch: it saves the thread's state and restores another's. Switching many times per second creates the appearance of simultaneous running, called concurrency; real parallelism needs several cores.",
      "With virtual memory, each process sees its own private range of addresses. The hardware's memory management unit translates these virtual addresses to physical memory using page tables kept by the OS, in fixed-size blocks called pages. One process therefore cannot read or overwrite another's memory, and pages that are not in use can be moved out to disk and brought back when needed, so programs can use more memory than is physically installed, at the cost of speed."
    ],
