        "concept": "Role of the operating system", "definition": "The OS manages hardware and shares it among programs; the privileged kernel acts on their behalf through system calls.",
        "facts": ["The kernel runs with full control of the hardware.", "Ordinary programs run in a restricted mode.", "Programs request services such as file access through system calls.", "This protects the system from faulty programs."],
        "misconceptions": ["The operating system is just the desktop and windows you see."] },
      { "key": "processes-threads", "title": "Distinguish processes from threads and explain race conditions", "description": "Explains separate memory for processes, shared memory for threads, and why locks are needed.",
        "concept": "Processes and threads", "definition": "A process is a running program with its own memory; threads within a process run separately but share its memory.",
        "facts": ["Each process has its own memory.", "Threads in one process share its memory.", "Unsynchronized updates to shared data cause race conditions.", "Locks let only one thread change shared data at a time."],
        "misconceptions": ["A program and a process are exactly the same thing.", "Threads of one process each get their own private copy of all memory."] },
      { "key": "scheduling", "title": "Explain how scheduling makes programs appear to run at once", "description": "Describes time slices and context switches and separates concurrency from parallelism.",
        "concept": "Scheduling and context switching", "definition": "The scheduler rapidly switches cores between threads, saving and restoring their state, so many make progress.",
        "facts": ["A core runs one thread at a time.", "The scheduler gives threads short time slices.", "A context switch saves one thread's state and restores another's.", "True parallelism requires multiple cores."],
        "misconceptions": ["A single-core computer runs all its programs at the exact same instant."] },
      { "key": "virtual-memory", "title": "Explain virtual memory", "description": "Explains private address spaces, page-table translation, isolation and paging to disk.",
        "concept": "Virtual memory", "definition": "Each process uses its own virtual addresses, which hardware maps to physical memory through OS-managed page tables.",
        "facts": ["Each process sees its own private address space.", "The memory management unit translates virtual to physical addresses using page tables.", "Processes cannot read or overwrite each other's memory.", "Unused pages can be moved to disk, at a cost in speed."],
        "misconceptions": ["Virtual memory is simply extra RAM that is as fast as real RAM."] }
    ],
