  {
    "title": "Binary and Data Representation",
    "category": "computing",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Data representation",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you to explain how computers represent numbers, text and other data using only bits. Work through small examples aloud, explain why, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Computers store everything as bits, each either 0 or 1, because electronic circuits can reliably tell two states apart. Binary is base 2: each position is worth twice the one to its right, so 1011 in binary is 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11. With n bits there are 2ⁿ different patterns; a byte of 8 bits has 256 patterns, enough for the whole numbers 0 to 255. Hexadecimal, base 16, is a compact way to write binary, because each hex digit stands for exactly four bits.",
      "Negative whole numbers are usually stored in two's complement. In an 8-bit two's complement number the leftmost bit carries a weight of −128 instead of +128, giving a range from −128 to 127. To negate a number you invert every bit and add one, and the same adding circuit then works for positive and negative numbers. Because the number of bits is fixed, a result that is too large wraps around, called overflow: adding 1 to 127 in 8-bit two's complement gives −128.",
      "Text is stored by giving each character a number. ASCII uses 7 bits for 128 characters, covering English letters, digits and punctuation. Unicode assigns a code point to well over a hundred thousand characters from the world's writing systems, including Korean Hangul and accented Spanish letters, and UTF-8 encodes each code point in 1 to 4 bytes, using a single byte for the ASCII characters.",
      "Real numbers are stored in floating point, a binary form of scientific notation with a sign, a fraction and an exponent. Only finitely many values can be represented, so most decimals are approximated: 0.1 has no exact binary representation, so adding 0.1 and 0.2 gives a result slightly different from 0.3. Images, sound and video are also just numbers, such as red, green and blue intensities for each pixel or the amplitude of a sound wave sampled many thousands of times per second; the same bits mean different things depending on how a program interprets them."
